-- title:
--   The first reduced modular unit has pole degree four
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be prime with $p=2$ or $p=3$, let $R$ be a level-$25$ reduction datum at $p$, let $κ_R$ be its residue field, let $F_R$ be the corresponding special function field, and let $U_0∈F_R$ be the first reduced modular unit. Then $F_R$ has degree four over the simple subfield generated by $U_0$:
--
--   $$[F_R:κ_R(U_0)]=4.$$
--
--   This exact pole degree is one of the two coprime degrees used to prove that the modular units generate the full special function field.
-- source:
--   B. Mazur, Modular curves and the Eisenstein ideal, Publ. IHES 47 (1977); Lean formalization of the order-25 case for this mission. Modules in the MazurProof/Keystone/Tate families are adapted from anthropics/fermats-last-theorem (Apache-2.0) via the xiangyazi24/FLT fork.

